// Comparison, sorting, and the closed set this whole repository spells as an `i32`.
//
// `../QUESTIONS.md`'s *a key and an order are different things* proposes a library type and says the
// language has to add nothing:
//
// > `struct SortSpec<T> { fn<i32(T, T)> key; fn<i32(T, T)> tieBreak; }` with `order()` composing
// > them and `sameKey()` exposed for `-u`, so a caller cannot reach the order when it wanted the
// > key. **The language has to add nothing.**
//
// This is that type written out. Writing it moved two things: the `i32` in that sketch is wrong for
// the reason this directory has recorded more often than any other, and replacing it costs something
// nobody has priced. §1 and §2.

import { Vec, Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * Less, the same, or greater — **the fifteenth closed set in this repository spelled as an `i32`,
 * and the first one that every sorting library in the world exports.**
 *
 * Counted over every package's `src`, plus `tools/` and `core/`: fifteen functions return a three-way
 * comparison and **all fifteen answer `i32`**, in eight packages. `cmp`, `cmpAbs`, `cmpBE`,
 * `cmpBytes`, `cmpNumeric`, `cmpNumericThenBytes`, `compareBytes`, `compareIndex`, `compareValue`,
 * `compareMidpoint`, `compareScaled`, `bigCmp` and three more. Not one returns an enum, and
 * `../QUESTIONS.md`'s *a closed set spelled as a string or an integer gives up a check the language
 * already makes* is the most repeated finding in this directory.
 *
 * The sign convention is the check being given up: a caller writing `if (cmp(a, b) < 0)` is reading a
 * documented sign, and `cmp(a, b) == -1` is the same intent written wrong against nine of the fifteen,
 * which return `a[i] - b[i]` unbounded.
 */
export enum Ordering {
  Less,
  Same,
  Greater,

  /** The `i32` convention, for a boundary that still wants it. Not for a caller inside this tree. */
  i32 sign(const this) {
    match (this) { Less: { return -1; } Same: { return 0; } Greater: { return 1; } }
  }

  /** From the convention. `n < 0`, not `n == -1` — see the doc comment above. */
  Ordering of(i32 n) { return n < 0 ? Less : (n > 0 ? Greater : Same); }
}

/**
 * A key and a total order over it, as one value.
 *
 * `key` answers *are these the same line* and `tieBreak` breaks equal keys. Composed, they are the
 * order a sort wants; separately, `sameKey` is what `sort -u` wants. A caller cannot reach one when
 * it meant the other, which is the whole of the type.
 */
export struct SortSpec<T> {
  fn<Ordering(T, T)> key;
  fn<Ordering(T, T)> tieBreak;

  /** The total order: the key, and the tie-break where the key is silent. */
  Ordering order(const this, T a, T b) {
    Ordering k = this.key(a, b);
    return k is Same ? this.tieBreak(a, b) : k;
  }

  /** Whether these are one line for the purpose of uniqueness. */
  bool sameKey(const this, T a, T b) { return this.key(a, b) is Same; }

  /** A spec with nothing to break ties with: the key is already total. */
  SortSpec<T> total(fn<Ordering(T, T)> cmp) { return SortSpec(cmp, cmp); }
}

/**
 * Sorted by `spec.order`, **stable** — and the stability is the decision, not the algorithm.
 *
 * Swept over the shipped tree: **thirteen functions named `*sort*` and eleven
 * `while (j >= 0 && …)` insertion-shift loops, across eight packages** — `wac`, `fs`, `sh`,
 * `wacpkg`, `tor`, `git`, `box`, `wacc`. Every one is an insertion sort or `box`'s merge sort, so
 * every one is stable, and three produce orderings a person reads: `box`'s `ls`, `box`'s `sort`, and
 * `git`'s tree listing.
 *
 * **An unstable `sort` in `core` would compile at all thirteen sites and change what three of them
 * print.** That is the whole reason this is a signature with a promise in it rather than a function
 * somebody adds when they need one — filed as `issues/system/0345a`, because the decision is the
 * shipped tree's rather than this directory's.
 */
export Vec<T> sorted<T>(const Vec<T> xs, const SortSpec<T> spec) { … }

/**
 * Adjacent equal **keys** collapsed, keeping the first — which is `sort -u` and is not
 * *adjacent equal values*.
 *
 * Takes a `Vec` already in `spec.order`. That precondition is the fifth thing in this directory a
 * type cannot say.
 */
export Vec<T> uniqueByKey<T>(const Vec<T> xs, const SortSpec<T> spec) { … }

/**
 * Lexicographic over bytes, shorter first on a prefix — **the same eight lines three packages
 * wrote.**
 *
 * `packages/tor/src/vote.wac`'s `compareBytes`, `packages/tor/src/hsdir.wac`'s `compareIndex` and
 * `packages/box/src/lib/lines.wac`'s `cmpBytes` are this function character for character apart from
 * parentheses and one temporary, and **two of the three are in one package**. `core/hash.wac` has
 * `bytesEq` and no `bytesCmp`, so equality is shared and ordering is not.
 */
export Ordering bytesCmp(const Bytes a, const Bytes b) {
  i32 n = a.len() < b.len() ? a.len() : b.len();
  for (i32 i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (a.get(i) != b.get(i)) { return a.get(i) < b.get(i) ? Less : Greater; }
  }
  if (a.len() == b.len()) { return Same; }
  return a.len() < b.len() ? Less : Greater;
}

// ## 1. The fourth byte comparator is not a duplicate, and that is the interesting one
//
// `packages/crypto/src/weierstrass.wac`'s `cmpBE` is the same *question* and a different function:
// big-endian, equal lengths assumed, and **branch-free** — no `return` inside the loop, two
// accumulators updated with masks, so it takes the same time whatever the bytes are.
//
// So `bytesCmp` above cannot replace all four, and **nothing in either signature says which one a
// caller needs.** `docs/` has a page on constant time and a type has no way to name it: the two
// functions differ in a property that is not in the return type, not in the parameters, and not
// checkable by any test that does not measure a clock.
//
// > **Three copies are a missing `core` function and the fourth is a missing type.** The first is a
// > chore; the second is the ask, and it is the *secret*-adjacent one this directory has five uses
// > of — a value whose handling is constrained by something the type carries.
//
// ## 2. And `Ordering` costs an allocation per comparison unless nobody says it does
//
// `../TECHNICAL.md` and `@/packages/wacc/src/emit.wac` between them settle what an enum is:
//
// > **An enum is one wasm struct**, not a base type with a subtype per variant … a tag in slot 0,
// > then a slot per payload field of every variant in declaration order.
//
// `Ordering` has three variants and no payload fields, so it is a struct of one `i32`. A comparator
// answering it does `struct.new` **per comparison**, and a sort of *n* items does that *n log n*
// times — in the inner loop of the one operation whose cost is the reason anybody writes a
// comparator by hand.
//
// A payload-free variant has no state, so three singletons could be allocated once and returned
// forever, which is what every language with sum types does. **wac does not, and
// `spec/spec/enums.md` says so while explaining something else:**
//
// > `Shape.Empty` on the right of `is` parses as an expression rather than a type, so the test became
// > **reference identity against a freshly constructed variant** and was always false
//
// *Always false* settles it. Against an interned value the comparison would have been right for
// exactly the cases the author meant; it was wrong for all of them, so every mention allocates.
//
// And that makes the ask a **change** rather than a missing sentence: interning would make reference
// identity meaningful for payload-free variants where today it never is, and both the
// qualified-variant rule and *"`is` does not narrow"* are written around the current behaviour.
//
// So the honest position on §above: **replacing fifteen `i32`s with an enum is the right change and
// it is not free**, and how unfree it is depends on a compiler decision nobody has stated. That is
// the first time in this directory that *a closed set spelled as an integer* has met a reason to
// stay an integer.
//
// ## 2b. And a doc comment cannot contain a glob, which this file found by not parsing
//
// The first draft of the `Ordering` comment above said *"Counted over `packages/*/src`, `tools/` and
// `core/`"* inside a `/** … */`, and `packages/*/src` contains `*/`, so the comment ended in the
// middle of a path and the lexer refused at the next backtick: **`stray '`' at 20:32`**.
//
// Which is ordinary C-family behaviour and is worth a line because of the asymmetry it creates
// **here specifically**: this directory writes `packages/*/src` constantly, it is legal in a `//`
// comment and fatal in a `/** */` one, and the two are used for different jobs — line comments for
// the argument, doc comments for the type. So the form that documents the *type* is the one that
// cannot name the corpus the type was derived from.
//
// The diagnostic is also worth keeping. It names the backtick, which is three characters after the
// real fault and on a line the author did not touch, because by then the lexer is reading prose as
// code. *`An unterminated block comment`* is what the same lexer says when the comment never closes;
// there is no message for one that closes **early**, and there cannot be, since a comment that ends
// where a `*/` appears is exactly what the rule says.
//
// ## 2c. And `core`'s own list of what is missing names two packages that do not sort
//
// `core/README.md`: *"**`sort`.** A generic sort over a `fn[bool(T, T)]` comparator belongs here.
// `gzip` and `bignum` both sort by hand today."* Checked: `packages/bignum/src` has no sort at all,
// and `packages/gzip`'s is a **counting sort** inside Huffman table construction — no comparator, and
// a generic `sort` would not serve it. Eight other packages do sort by hand and the sentence names
// none of them.
//
// Both halves of that sentence are also wrong about the *type*: `fn[bool(T, T)]` cannot express a
// key, which is the whole of the entry this file is written from. A `bool` says *which comes first*
// and `sort -u` needs *are these the same line*.
//
// **The entry above it in the same list is right, and the difference is that somebody checked.**
// *"**`Set<T>`.** `Map<T, bool>` is the whole implementation, and a wrapper is worth writing when
// something wants one."* Counted: `Map<T, bool>` appears **zero** times in the tree, and `Map` at all
// in six files. Nothing wants a set, the deferral is correct, and the sentence describes an
// implementation nobody has reached for — which is what a *not yet* entry should look like. Two
// entries, one section, one checked and one not.
//
// Worth recording as a fact about lists rather than about sorting: **a *what is not here yet* section
// is a claim about the rest of the tree, and nothing checks it.** This one has been wrong in three
// ways for as long as it has existed, and finding that out cost two greps.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`Vec<T>` has no `set`, so `sorted` cannot sort in place.** The surface is `create`, `len`, `get`,
// `push`, `pop`, `last`, `items` — read and append, and one removal from the end. So the signature
// above answers a **new** `Vec`, which allocates the whole thing again, in the function most likely
// to be handed something large. First place a `core` type's own surface blocks a `core` function,
// and it is a five-line addition rather than a language question — which is why it is here rather
// than in `../QUESTIONS.md`.
//
// **The refinement law is uncheckable.** `spec.key(a, b) is not Same` must imply `spec.order(a, b)`
// agrees with it, and that is what makes `sort -nu` correct. Fifth law in this directory after
// `hash`/`eq`, `combine`'s associativity, `Key`'s, and `missingFrom` against `assignable`. Every one
// of the five is a relation between two functions and the language has no way to state any of them.
//
// **A key that allocates is a key you cannot use, and the type cannot say so.**
// `@/packages/box/src/lib/lines.wac`'s `leadingNumber` saturates at `i64` rather than reaching for
// `@/packages/bignum`, and the shipped file's reason is a measured bug in `sort -n`. The constraint
// is that a comparator runs *n log n* times, so it must not allocate — the same class of statement
// as constant-time in §1, unsayable for the same reason, and this file's own `Ordering` breaks it.
//
// **`uniqueByKey` takes a `Vec` that must already be ordered and cannot say so.** The name says it
// and the type does not, which is *a value whose guarantee comes from how it was made* at its eighth
// witness — and here the guarantee comes from **another function having been called first**, which is
// weaker than the seven before it: `Prefix` and `Digest32` are built by the thing that checks them,
// and a sorted `Vec` is an ordinary `Vec` that has been somewhere.
